Since $\overrightarrow {a}= \overrightarrow {b}+ \overrightarrow {c}$, we can express $\overrightarrow {c}$ as $\overrightarrow {c}= \overrightarrow {a}- \overrightarrow {b}$.

Taking squares on both sides, we get:
\begin{align*}
\overrightarrow {c}^2 &= (\overrightarrow {a}- \overrightarrow {b})^2 \\
&= \overrightarrow {a}^2 - 2 \overrightarrow {a}\cdot \overrightarrow {b} + \overrightarrow {b}^2.
