Now we will use the scalar product of vectors (dot product) to express the cosine of the angle between two vectors. The dot product of two vectors $\overrightarrow{a}$ and $\overrightarrow{b}$ is given by:
\begin{align*}
\overrightarrow {a}\cdot \overrightarrow {b} &= |\overrightarrow {a}|\ |\overrightarrow {b}|\cos\theta.
\end{align*}

Substituting this into our square identity, we have:
\begin{align*}
|\overrightarrow {c}|^2 &= |\overrightarrow {a}|^2 - 2|\overrightarrow {a}|\ |\overrightarrow {b}|\cos\theta + |\overrightarrow {b}|^2,
\end{align*}
where $|\overrightarrow {a}|$, $|\overrightarrow {b}|$, and $|\overrightarrow {c}|$ are the magnitudes of the vectors $\overrightarrow {a}$, $\overrightarrow {b}$, and $\overrightarrow {c}$ respectively.

The missing steps above might include the actual calculations of the magnitudes and the use of any given information to find the value of $\cos\theta$. Since the provided solution and the problem do not give specific values or conditions to determine $\theta$, we are unable to proceed further without additional information. Therefore, we reach an impasse in finding the numerical value of $\theta$, but we have laid out the method to find it given the magnitudes of vectors and their dot product.

\begin{align*}
