Given the function $f(x)=|x-2|+|x+1|$  
(I) Solve the inequality $f(x) \geqslant 4-x$ with respect to $x$;  
(II) If $a, b \in \{y|y=f(x)\}$, compare the magnitude of $2(a+b)$ and $ab+4$.

s:
(I) When $x < -1$, $f(x)=1-2x$, and $f(x) \geqslant 4-x$ becomes $1-2x \geqslant 4-x$, which solves to $x \leqslant -3$. Thus, the solution is $x \leqslant -3$;  
When $-1 \leqslant x \leqslant 2$, $f(x)=3$, and $f(x) \geqslant 4-x$ becomes $3 \geqslant 4-x$, which solves to $x \geqslant 1$. Thus, the solution is $1 \leqslant x \leqslant 2$;  
When $x > 2$, $f(x)=2x-1$, and $f(x) \geqslant 4-x$ becomes $2x-1 \geqslant 4-x$, which solves to $x \geqslant \frac{5}{3}$. Thus, the solution is $x > 2$.  
Combining all cases, we get $x \geqslant 1$ or $x \leqslant -3$.  
Therefore, the solution set is $(-\infty, -3] \cup [1, +\infty)$;  

(II) Since $f(x) \geqslant 3$, we have $a \geqslant 3$ and $b \geqslant 3$,  
$2(a+b)-(ab+4)=2a-ab+2b-4=(a-2)(2-b)$,  
Since $a \geqslant 3$ and $b \geqslant 3$, we have $a-2 > 0$ and $2-b < 0$,  
which implies $(a-2)(2-b) < 0$,  
Therefore, $2(a+b) < ab+4$.  

Thus, the final answers are:  
(I) The solution set is $\boxed{(-\infty, -3] \cup [1, +\infty)}$;  
(II) $2(a+b) < ab+4$ is $\boxed{\text{True}}$.

