The graph of $|f(x)|$ is symmetric about the axis. When $x_1$ and $x_2$ are symmetric about the axis of $|f(x)|$, clearly $x_1 \neq x_2 + k\pi$ ($k \in \mathbb{Z}$). Therefore, statement ② is incorrect;  
When $x \in \left[- \frac {\pi}{4}, \frac {\pi}{4}\right]$, $\cos x > 0$, thus $f(x) = \cos x \cdot \sin x = \frac {1}{2} \sin 2x$. During this time, $2x \in \left[- \frac {\pi}{2}, \frac {\pi}{2}\right]$, therefore $f(x)$ is monotonically increasing in the interval $\left[- \frac {\pi}{4}, \frac {\pi}{4}\right]$. Hence, statement ③ is correct;  
Since $f\left(- \frac {\pi}{4}\right) = - \frac {1}{2}$ and $f\left( \frac {3\pi}{4}\right) = \frac {1}{2}$, the smallest positive period of the function $f(x)$ is not $\pi$. Therefore, statement ④ is incorrect;  
Since $|\cos x| \geq 0$, the center of symmetry for $f(x)$ is the same as the center of symmetry for $y = \sin x$. Therefore, statement ⑤ is correct;  
Thus, the answer is: $\boxed{\text{①③⑤}}$.  
